  "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B056",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B034",
    "B055"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I have changed my preferred configuration to B056. Optical lattice clocks connect a decisive physical idea to sustained experimental realization, with performance independently established across laboratories. Katori's magic-wavelength lattice concept and Ye's foundational experimental development form a compact and intelligible allocation. The clocks have made precision measurement a durable new means for fundamental tests as well as metrology; unlike an elegant proposal awaiting nature's verdict, this achievement has repeatedly met nature in the laboratory.",
    "case_against": "The historical boundary of the prize must be drawn tightly: other major contributors belong to the wider optical-clock enterprise, and the full downstream consequences for time standards and fundamental discoveries are still unfolding. The award should recognize the lattice-clock invention and development, not claim credit for every later optical-clock advance.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's experimental development produced an independently reproducible measurement capability, while noting the need to assess wider field credit.",
        "response": "I agree. This combination of a distinctive principle, lasting realization, and cross-laboratory validation is precisely what moves an instrument from technical promise to a mature physical achievement. His attribution caution should remain part of the final historical review, but it does not weaken the central two-person case."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce prefers B056 because its durable measurement capability and compact concept-to-instrument attribution are more sharply delimited than collaboration-scale astronomical achievements.",
        "response": "I find that comparison persuasive. A prize need not wait for every eventual application, but it must identify an achievement and its responsible people with confidence. B056 currently offers a more secure route to both than B055, whose remarkable observational opening still poses collaboration-scale credit and inference-boundary problems."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson maintains that quantum error correction merits recognition for its foundational physical principle despite the absence of scalable fault-tolerant computation.",
        "response": "The principle is indeed profound, and I do not regard incomplete applications as automatically disqualifying. But the present absence of scalable fault tolerance leaves the maturity judgment less settled than for B056. The lattice clocks have already become reproducible instruments whose core achievement does not depend on a future threshold being crossed."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that B023 is a mature foundational achievement, provided its two historically distinct phase discoveries can be united under a sufficiently narrow citation.",
        "response": "His proposed narrow framing is thoughtful, but the remaining need to join distinct histories and assess excluded contributors is substantive. B056 has less conceptual sweep, yet its prize-defined achievement is more singular and its allocation presently more defensible."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether documentation can establish that Katori and Ye alone adequately delimit the decisive invention and development of optical lattice clocks.",
      "Whether the observational successes of inflation can ultimately be judged sufficiently discriminating to restore B034 above this experimentally consolidated alternative."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
